The rest of Go's standard library on Radix (ADR 0350): the C library's
ways that macOS and OpenBSD take (the *at functions, writev, accept4,
pipe2, getrandom) with Linux's structures (stat, dirent, socket
options); time zones from /system/resources/zoneinfo and root
certificates from /system/resources/certificates; the host name from
uname; the program's path from the runtime. net lists the interfaces of
/kernel/class/net (radix-sysfs(5), ADR 0355), with their addresses but not
their multicast groups, and has neither sendfile nor splice, which the C
library lacks. It resolves names in Go in a program with cgo too, as the
C library has no res_search (ADR 0357). Its tests name Radix among the
systems that have writev, that have no SOCK_SEQPACKET sockets, and whose
interfaces list no multicast addresses. Dukovo only.

